+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service or weighs a trade-off no decision record settles. These tenets guide proposals
+before implementation. Written after the service argument recurred in [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), following
+the session-store and background-job discussions.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Prefer what we already run over one more service
+
+Redis was rejected for sessions because only one volunteer would have run it; the
+[session decision](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) chose Postgres. Later, Redis holding the job queue lost
+receipt jobs and again depended on one volunteer for recovery; the
+[background-job decision](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) moved jobs to Postgres. Those decisions apply
+this tenet to separate needs.
+
+Larder's operating baseline is one database and one web app. A proposal for another service the volunteers would
+operate must show both a need Postgres cannot meet and two volunteers able to restore the proposed service. This
+rules out adding a service just because it is a familiar or convenient tool for the job, or because one volunteer
+can set it up over a weekend.
+
+In issue 70, pantry search for "rice" reportedly took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown". Elasticsearch was
+proposed, but Postgres full-text search on the item name brought the reported time to 40 ms and found the missing
+item. That removed the need for another service in that proposal.
+
+This allows external services: the card gateway is already outside larder, and a hosted service that volunteers
+do not have to operate is an acceptable option. For a new service volunteers would operate, start the proposal
+with the unmet need and the two people able to restore it.
